# Nidara identifies itself as Nidara and says, in the field that exists for
# exactly that, that it IS an Arch system. Both halves matter: the name is ours
# (rebrand), the lineage is Arch's and stated as such (no appropriation). Arch's
# name and logo are trademarks; "based on Arch Linux" is nominative use and
# ID_LIKE="arch" is the same statement in a form scripts can read.
#
# This file is the LIVE medium's alone, and it is deliberately not a package.
#
# The installed system gets its identity from `nidara-release` (built from
# ../../../packages/nidara-release), which OWNS /etc/os-release so the product's
# version can travel by `pacman -Syu`. That package is not on this image: an
# airootfs overlay would overwrite the file it owns and leave `pacman -Qkk`
# calling the medium tampered with for as long as it runs.
#
# So the two files differ on purpose, in two ways. PRETTY_NAME says "(live)",
# which is worth being able to see in a bug report; and there is NO VERSION here,
# because the product's number is declared in one place (../../../VERSION) and a
# hand-maintained copy is a copy that can disagree. The medium's own version is
# the one in its filename.
NAME="Nidara"
PRETTY_NAME="Nidara (live)"
ID=nidara
ID_LIKE="arch"
BUILD_ID=rolling
ANSI_COLOR="0;36"
HOME_URL="https://github.com/nidara-project"
DOCUMENTATION_URL="https://github.com/nidara-project/nidara-desktop"
SUPPORT_URL="https://github.com/nidara-project/nidara-desktop/issues"
BUG_REPORT_URL="https://github.com/nidara-project/nidara-desktop/issues"
LOGO=nidara
